[{:db/id "q-109152/selection/6204a7f49d31d64de344570c382d077ad3a2c85aba589915cbcd380a42112a8e" :answer/id #uuid "47d10c09-d110-5618-87f1-9aaa11031ab0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109152/selection/17f7a92ecac965d3850a7da98658af322c6b691d4b3336f622586133c927298d" :answer/id #uuid "1ec757bb-3dfa-50cf-a036-4b701e244974" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109152/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer/id #uuid "182d89a3-8e68-56fd-99ea-cc5228a901c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109152/selection/a272cbe64fea514fe5c8c9685aef2c9378fdfe44c54941c8bcba64a5f713bc84" :answer/id #uuid "6f95090b-f1af-51e9-b801-724b96eb80e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109152/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "2f5efcdb-4fb6-575b-8c3e-cbfad4ea791d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109152/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "461fe2f6-5dcd-5d99-aa2b-10d5c980a09c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109152/selection/6204a7f49d31d64de344570c382d077ad3a2c85aba589915cbcd380a42112a8e" :answer-field/choices ["q-109152/selection/6204a7f49d31d64de344570c382d077ad3a2c85aba589915cbcd380a42112a8e" "q-109152/selection/17f7a92ecac965d3850a7da98658af322c6b691d4b3336f622586133c927298d" "q-109152/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" "q-109152/selection/a272cbe64fea514fe5c8c9685aef2c9378fdfe44c54941c8bcba64a5f713bc84" "q-109152/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109152" :question/id #uuid "c9378bf5-2695-5a6e-bd26-2cc8bb89f62a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109152" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,1)$ for the curve $5{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{4}) & =0 \\\\ 15{x}^{2}-8{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -8{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-15{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{8{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{8{y}^{3}})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot {1}^{2}}{8\\cdot {1}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109152/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109152"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65181"] :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}=\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-332518/field-1/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9" :answer/id #uuid "d32814f1-d1e0-50b7-89a9-a00a0bc611b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332518/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f38c1260-42a1-543e-88d9-b37d2938d87a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332518/field-1/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9" :answer-field/choices ["q-332518/field-1/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332518/field-2/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9" :answer/id #uuid "05ebb977-398f-5685-b816-3d967ddaddb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332518/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb72f22-101f-5fc1-9ed5-76b535fd3701" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332518/field-2/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9" :answer-field/choices ["q-332518/field-2/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332518" :question/id #uuid "2045d655-d8e8-53aa-8739-aa078fd23538" :question/math-academy-id "q-332518" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-332518/field-1" "q-332518/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332518"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-12019"] :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-3x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3} & =3\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-2)}{u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-3x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-3x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-3x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-3x)+0 \\\\  & =-(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3(1-3x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-332057/field-1/1ef14df341e2f354c34ae93e3c7ddfea65840e9fda1897b5ea50de018c5615fe" :answer/id #uuid "680f309c-f29c-5d04-b772-47562f3b779d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332057/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d09aa2-650f-5b7c-a37d-af9190217b56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-332057/field-1/1ef14df341e2f354c34ae93e3c7ddfea65840e9fda1897b5ea50de018c5615fe" :answer-field/choices ["q-332057/field-1/1ef14df341e2f354c34ae93e3c7ddfea65840e9fda1897b5ea50de018c5615fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332057" :question/id #uuid "48d0e4ae-cc5c-5722-bf40-db66985016c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332057" :question/problem "A data set has a mean $\\overset{―}{x}=6.2$ and an unknown standard deviation. An observation of the data set has the value $x=5.08$ and $z$-score $z=-0.7.$ Find the standard deviation of the data set.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5.08,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=6.2,$ and $z=-0.7$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.08 & =6.2+(-0.7)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -1.12 & =-0.7\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332057/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332057"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65951"] :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-1$ and adding $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\ge 1+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-1$ and adding $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\le -1+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 2,\\,f(x)\\le 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-344110/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer/id #uuid "5bfc4274-fe83-5def-b030-97c4e642e606" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344110/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "02590595-12a1-572b-b24b-b038aa655c21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344110/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer-field/choices ["q-344110/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344110" :question/id #uuid "1f95a9f2-42f7-5f98-8ff5-f6c60ffeb68d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344110" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/assets/1b564f91cb71a17a1449e1ff723e32f450da6304d9c3ba33cd01e2ff942bc25c.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344110/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e37b91f0-ea0a-58b0-98d0-8f3626578dd6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344110"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
